
  <sn><md>:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
### The OSI Model

- The **OSI (`Open Systems Interconnection`) model** is a `conceptual framework`, published by `ISO` in `1984`, that `splits network communication` into **seven layers**. 
- Each layer has one job, talks only to the layers directly above and below it, and communicates with the matching layer on the other machine. 
- It's mostly a `teaching and troubleshooting` tool. Real networks run on the simpler `TCP/IP` model, but OSI vocabulary ("Layer 2 switch," "Layer 7 firewall") is used everywhere.
- The OSI model serves as a conceptual blueprint that defines how data moves between two devices across a network, `regardless of their underlying hardware` architecture or operating system.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://assets.gcore.pro/site-media/uploads/what_is_osi_fi_4c4cb6e45d.png)
:::
:::

