:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
#### 7. Application Layer
- **Function:** Provides network services directly to end-user applications.
- **Examples:** HTTP, FTP, SMTP, DNS, Telnet
- **Analogy:** The interface where you interact (web browser, email client)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
#### 6. Presentation Layer
- **Function:** Translates, encrypts, and compresses data so it's readable by the application layer.
- **Examples:** SSL/TLS encryption, JPEG, GIF, MPEG, ASCII/Unicode conversion
- **Analogy:** A translator ensuring both sides speak the same language
:::
:::


:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
#### 5. Session Layer
- **Function:** Establishes, manages, and terminates sessions (connections) between applications.
- **Examples:** RPC, NetBIOS, session tokens
- **Analogy:** A phone operator managing the start and end of a call
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
#### 4. Transport Layer
- **Function:** Provides reliable (or unreliable) end-to-end data delivery, segmentation, flow control, and error recovery.
- **Protocols:** TCP (reliable), UDP (fast, unreliable)
- **Key Concepts:** Port numbers, segmentation, acknowledgments
- **Analogy:** A postal service ensuring packages arrive intact and in order
:::
:::



:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
#### 3. Network Layer
- **Function:** Handles logical addressing and routing of data between networks.
- **Protocols:** IP (IPv4/IPv6), ICMP, ARP, routers
- **Key Concepts:** IP addresses, routing, packets
- **Analogy:** A GPS system finding the best route between cities
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
#### 2. Data Link Layer
- **Function:** Provides node-to-node data transfer, error detection, and physical addressing.
- **Protocols:** Ethernet, PPP, MAC, switches
- **Key Concepts:** MAC addresses, frames, error detection (CRC)
- **Analogy:** A local delivery truck moving packages between nearby stops
:::
:::


#### 1. Physical Layer
- **Function:** Transmits raw bitstreams (0s and 1s) over physical media.
- **Examples:** Cables (twisted pair, fiber), wireless signals, hubs, voltages, pin layouts
- **Analogy:** The actual roads and wires the data travels on
</md></sn><sn><md>### The Seven Layers

![img](https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcS90HSxShmQjd3yCphemVKj2N1bX-k21CFCV_vZtKQy1w&amp;s=10)

+++ ...
![img](https://www.infosectrain.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/OSI-Model-Layers-1.jpg)
+++

| # | Layer | What it does | Data unit | Examples |
|---|-------|--------------|-----------|----------|
| 7 | **Application** | `Interface` for network services used by software | Data | HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, SMTP, DNS |
| 6 | **Presentation** | `Translates, encrypts, and compresses` data | Data | TLS/SSL, JPEG, ASCII, MPEG |
| 5 | **Session** | `Opens, manages, and closes` sessions between applications | Data | NetBIOS, RPC, session tokens |
| 4 | **Transport** | End-to-end delivery, segmentation, error recovery, flow control | Segment (TCP) / Datagram (UDP) | TCP, UDP |
| 3 | **Network** | Logical addressing and routing between networks | Packet | IP, ICMP, routers |
| 2 | **Data Link** | `Node-to-node` delivery on the same network, MAC addressing, error detection | Frame | Ethernet, Wi-Fi (802.11), switches |
| 1 | **Physical** | `Raw bit transmission` over a medium | Bits | Cables, fiber, hubs, radio signals |

A common mnemonic, top to bottom: **"All People Seem To Need Data Processing."** Bottom to top: **"Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away."**

+++ ...
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/osi_model_neumonics.png)
+++</md></sn><sn><md>### How Data Flows

When you send data, it moves **down** the stack on your machine. Each layer adds its own header, a process called `encapsulation`. The bits travel across the medium, and the receiving machine moves them **up** the stack, with each layer stripping its header (`decapsulation`).

For example, loading a web page: 

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/how_data_flow_diagram.png)

- your browser creates an HTTP request (Layer 7), 
- TLS encrypts it (6), 
- TCP splits it into segments (4), 
- IP adds source and destination addresses (3), 
- Ethernet or Wi-Fi wraps it in frames (2), and 
- the network card sends it as signals (1).
</md></sn><sn><md>### OSI vs. TCP/IP

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
- TCP/IP has four layers: Application (OSI 5-7), Transport (4), Internet (3), and Network Access (1-2). 
- OSI is the `reference model` for understanding, while TCP/IP is what the internet actually uses.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
![img](https://images.shiksha.com/mediadata/ugcDocuments/images/wordpressImages/2022_03_OSI-Model-vs-TCP-IP-Model-Difference.jpg)
:::
:::

+++ ...

| OSI (7 layers) | TCP/IP (4 layers) |
|---|---|
| Application, Presentation, Session | Application |
| Transport | Transport |
| Network | Internet |
| Data Link, Physical | Network Access |

The **TCP/IP model** is what's actually implemented on the internet, while OSI remains a reference/teaching model.

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/osi_tcp_model_diagram.png)
+++

</md></sn><sn><md>### Why It Matters

- **Troubleshooting:** You can isolate problems `layer by layer`. 
  - A dead cable is Layer 1, 
  - a wrong IP is Layer 3, 
  - a blocked port is Layer 4.
- **Standardization:** Vendors can build interoperable products as long as they follow each layer's interface.
- **Modularity:** A layer can change (say, Wi-Fi replacing Ethernet) without affecting the others.
- **Design:** Guides how protocols and hardware are developed.
- **Education:** Makes complex networking concepts easier to understand.

</md></sn>